  schema:text "Previously, Avenell et al. (2019) concluded that the findings of 13/36 (36%) systematic reviews, clinical guidelines and other reviews would likely change if the retracted trials they cited were removed. Referring to: Avenell, A., Stewart, F., Grey, A., Gamble, G., & Bolland, M. (2019). An investigation into the impact and implications of published papers from retracted research: Systematic search of affected literature. BMJ Open, 9(10), e031909. However, in Table 2 of Avenell et al. (2019), it is indicated that the findings of 13/32 (or ~41%) of the findings of affected publications would likely change. Could the authors please check if it supposed to be 13/32 or 13/36, and thus, 36% or 41%.";
  ripe:concerns <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/work/10.1080%2F08989621.2022.2082290>;
  tido:supports <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-human-1-2>,
    <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-inspectai-1-2> .

<https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/peer-comment/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-2> a ripe:PeerComment;
  dc:creator <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/author/pubpeer-RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-2>;
  dc:date "October 2022";
  fabio:hasURL "https://pubpeer.com/publications/16FE217615B0BBB47C11745DCA7DA2"^^xsd:anyURI;
  prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/source/pubpeer>;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/evidence-aggregation/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA>;
  prov:wasMemberOf <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/research-integrity-assessment/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA>;
  schema:text "PS 2022 paper: \"Following suspicions aroused during the conduct of a Cochrane review (Avenell et al. 2009), 33 randomized trials by Yoshihiro Sato and Jun Iwamoto were investigated, and findings published (Bolland et al. 2016). Of these publications, twenty-eight were retracted between 2016–2019 in the field of osteoporosis\" 2019 paper: \"By May 2019, 27/33 of these affected trial reports had been retracted\" Were 27 or 28 papers retracted?";
  ripe:concerns <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/work/10.1080%2F08989621.2022.2082290>;
  tido:supports <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-human-1-2>,
    <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-inspectai-1-2> .

<https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/peer-comment/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-3> a ripe:PeerComment;
  dc:creator <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/author/pubpeer-RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-3>;
  dc:date "October 2022";
  fabio:hasURL "https://pubpeer.com/publications/16FE217615B0BBB47C11745DCA7DA2"^^xsd:anyURI;
  prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/source/pubpeer>;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/evidence-aggregation/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA>;
  prov:wasMemberOf <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/research-integrity-assessment/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA>;
  schema:text "PS 2 \"By August 2021, 72 citing publications listed by WoS themselves had 10,493 citations, and 10 unlisted by WoS had 424 citations on Scopus. Of these 82 citing publications, 67 (82%) had at least one citation in 2021.\" Do the authors mean 72, or 82?";
  ripe:concerns <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/work/10.1080%2F08989621.2022.2082290>;
  tido:supports <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-human-1-2>,
    <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-inspectai-1-2> .

<https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/peer-comment/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-4> a ripe:PeerComment;
  dc:creator <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/author/pubpeer-RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-4>;
  dc:date "October 2022";
  fabio:hasURL "https://pubpeer.com/publications/16FE217615B0BBB47C11745DCA7DA2"^^xsd:anyURI;
  prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/source/pubpeer>;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/evidence-aggregation/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA>;
  prov:wasMemberOf <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/research-integrity-assessment/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA>;
  schema:text "Thank you for your questions and interest in our papers. Thank you for spotting my error in our 2022 paper. In our 2019 BMJ Open paper, in Table 2 13/32 (which is 41%) of affected papers had findings that would likely change. In our 2022 paper this is incorrectly stated as 13/36 (36%). I will ask Accountability in Research to correct our 2022 paper. The 2019 BMJ Open paper is correct – by April 2019, 27/33 of affected trial reports had been retracted. One further affected trial report was retracted later in 2019. Thus the 2022 paper is also correct, as the literature search took place later in 2019. In the 2022 paper, the number is 82: 72 publications were listed on Web of Science and an additional 10 publications were listed on Scopus but not listed on Web of Science, of which 67/82 (82%) had at least one citation in 2021. Alison Avenell";
  ripe:concerns <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/work/10.1080%2F08989621.2022.2082290>;
  tido:supports <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-human-1-2>,
    <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DBDE4E81DF5F0FA-inspectai-1-2> .

<https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/peer-comment/RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F-1> a ripe:PeerComment;
  dc:creator <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/author/pubpeer-RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F-1>;
  dc:date "May 2021";
  fabio:hasURL "https://pubpeer.com/publications/473737BEFA1E6B814531F7CEF8D3BB"^^xsd:anyURI;
  prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/source/pubpeer>;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/evidence-aggregation/RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F>;
  prov:wasMemberOf <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/research-integrity-assessment/RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F>;
  schema:text """There are many concerns about the integrity of 172 publications by this research group, including this one. Some of the concerns are published
1. Anaesthesia 2020;75:1683–92, see Supplementary data. This paper is A149
2. Account Res 2020;28:149-61.
3. J Clin Epidemiol 2021;131:22-29.
Journals and publishers were notified of these concerns in July 2019. 18 publications have an expression of concern, and 6 have been retracted. To our knowledge there has been no public response about any of the concerns. We think these publications are unreliable""";
  ripe:concerns <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/work/10.1007%2Fs12011-017-1085-0>;
  tido:supports <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F-human-1-2>,
    <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F-inspectai-1-2> .

<https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/peer-comment/RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F-2> a ripe:PeerComment;
  dc:creator <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/author/pubpeer-RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F-2>;
  dc:date "November 2022";
  fabio:hasURL "https://pubpeer.com/publications/473737BEFA1E6B814531F7CEF8D3BB"^^xsd:anyURI;
  prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/source/pubpeer>;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/evidence-aggregation/RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F>;
  prov:wasMemberOf <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/research-integrity-assessment/RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F>;
  schema:text """Retracted [19 November 2022](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12011-022-03465-3). The Editors-in-Chief have retracted this article. After publication, several concerns have been raised about the integrity of this article. The article overlaps with another publication by the same author group [1] in terms of reported study timelines, location and sample size, but there is no cross-referencing. The following discrepancies were noted between the two studies: * This article does not mention any metformin treatment, whereas [1] reports that participants were taking 500 mg of metformin per day, increasing to 1500 mg/day over the first 3 weeks.
* This article does not mention stratification in the article, whereas [1] indicates that participants were matched for BMI, age, phenotypes A and D and then randomized.
* Smokers are only listed as an exclusion criterion in the current article. In addition, this article appears to contain some data errors. * Under the “Study Design” section, it is stated that “Forty PCOS women were randomized (…)”, however, the article describes 60 participants elsewhere.
* In the first paragraph of the “Results” section, it is erroneously stated that 60 participants in each group completed the trial. Furthermore, the authors had previously published studies which reported that zinc and selenium supplementation improved clinical outcomes in women with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) [2, 3]. As this clinical trial includes a placebo-controlled arm, concerns have been raised that potential beneficial treatments known to authors appear to have been withheld from this treatment group. An investigation into all the concerns was conducted by the National Committee for Ethics in Biomedical Research (NREC), Ministry of Health and Medical Education. The NREC concluded that there is insufficient evidence that the data was fabricated based on the summary statistics. In line with Iranian legal requirements, the results of their investigation were sent to the Kashan University of Medical Sciences (KAUMS) research ethics committee where Dr. Asemi is based. Of the specific concerns raised in relation to this article, the institution and authors confirmed that both this article and [1] were part of the same project, including inclusion and exclusion criteria, metformin treatment, and stratification. The authors stated that the two studies were differentiated by selecting different primary outcomes based on different biochemical variables. Further, the authors would like to correct the error within their article and confirm that the study included a total of 60 participants. The Editors have determined that the conclusions of this study are unreliable due to omissions in the description of the trial and the selective analysis of the same data set which was presented earlier in [1]. The authors Mehri Jamilian and Zatollah Asemi disagree with this retraction. The author Maryam Maktami has not responded to correspondence regarding this retraction. **References**
* [1] Jamilian M, Maktabi M, Asemi Z (2017) A trial on the effects of magnesium-zinc-calcium-vitamin D co-supplementation on glycemic control and markers of cardio-metabolic risk in women with polycystic ovary syndrome. Arch Iran Med 20(10):640–645
* [2] Jamilian M et al (2016) Effects of zinc supplementation on endocrine outcomes in women with polycystic ovary syndrome: a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial. Biol Trace Elem Res 170(2):271–278
* [3] Razavi M et al (2016) Selenium supplementation and the effects on reproductive outcomes, biomarkers of inflammation, and oxidative stress in women with polycystic ovary syndrome. Horm Metab Res 48(3):185–190""";
  ripe:concerns <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/work/10.1007%2Fs12011-017-1085-0>;
  tido:supports <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F-human-1-2>,
    <https://w3id.org/ripe/ripe-kg/integrity-assessment-hypothesis/RIPEA6DC28A51A5E4686F-inspectai-1-2> .

